INT. VICTORIAN OBSERVATORY - NIGHT (1887)

A copper dome open to the stars. A great brass telescope on a creaking mount, its tube pointing at a smear of the Milky Way. Chalk equations cover a slate board.

A YOUNG WOMAN in a plain gray dress (20s) stands at the eyepiece, a notebook clutched to her chest. She has slipped in through the coal door. Behind her, from the stairwell, a lantern rises.

An old man, PROFESSOR EUSTACE PEMBERTON (70s, nightshirt, dressing gown, white whiskers), emerges, glaring.

PROFESSOR
Who is there? Show yourself!

She spins, notebook falling. He lifts the lantern. The light finds her face.

YOUNG WOMAN
Professor, please. I only wished to see.

PROFESSOR
This is a private instrument.

YOUNG WOMAN
I am Ottoline Marsh, sir. I wrote to you eleven times.

He lowers the lantern a little. His beard trembles.

PROFESSOR
The scullery girl's cousin? The one who counts comets?

OTTOLINE
I do not count them, sir. I find them.

He stares at her. Then at the telescope. Then at the sky through the open dome.

PROFESSOR
Show me.

She hesitates, steps to the eyepiece, adjusts a brass knob with practiced fingers. He peers in after her. A long silence.

PROFESSOR (CONT'D)
God in heaven.

OTTOLINE
Yes, sir.
